One acceptable answer was to never “drop” a decoded packet simply because no device session is found. In our original code the very last lines were

  if (deviceSession != null) {
     for (Position p : positions) {
     p.setDeviceId(deviceSession.getDeviceId());
     }
  } else {
     return null;
  }
  return positions;

This meant that even when a valid packet had been parsed the decode(…) method would return null (causing the “list is null” error) if deviceSession was still null. One correct fix was to remove the “else return null” so that the method always returns the decoded list. For example:

